1
00:00:09,389 --> 00:00:15,434
Now let's dive into network
namespaces a little more.

2
00:00:15,434 --> 00:00:21,496
So, just as a recap,
we've seen this picture before.

3
00:00:21,496 --> 00:00:26,924
So kind of recapping the kind
of progression of technology.

4
00:00:26,924 --> 00:00:32,157
So originally we have bare metal, and
when we refer to bare metal we're

5
00:00:32,157 --> 00:00:36,871
just talking about the hardware
running an operating system and

6
00:00:36,871 --> 00:00:40,205
you want to run some
application on top of it.

7
00:00:40,205 --> 00:00:45,084
So the problem with that
was there's some resource

8
00:00:45,084 --> 00:00:50,424
wastage if you're only running
one application on it.

9
00:00:50,424 --> 00:00:54,556
So people want to run multiple
applications to make best use of

10
00:00:54,556 --> 00:00:57,317
the hardware that they're paying for.

11
00:00:57,317 --> 00:01:01,897
But the problem is that these aren't
kind of isolated environments.

12
00:01:01,897 --> 00:01:06,939
You might have dependencies and things
like that, that might be hard to resolve.

13
00:01:06,939 --> 00:01:12,518
Maybe it's requiring different
versions of operating systems or

14
00:01:12,518 --> 00:01:16,009
different packages or things like that.

15
00:01:16,009 --> 00:01:19,874
So we then move to virtual machines,

16
00:01:19,874 --> 00:01:25,108
which is a technology where
you run a hypervisor,

17
00:01:25,108 --> 00:01:32,852
that's a layer of software that
effectively mimics physical hardware.

18
00:01:32,852 --> 00:01:37,395
You run an operating system on
top of it and it makes use of, or

19
00:01:37,395 --> 00:01:40,427
it looks like to that operating system,

20
00:01:40,427 --> 00:01:45,082
it's running on hardware that
it's the only thing running.

21
00:01:45,082 --> 00:01:51,076
And what this provides is
the ability to have this isolation.

22
00:01:51,076 --> 00:01:53,404
But it's a bit heavyweight, right?

23
00:01:53,404 --> 00:01:57,503
So you've seen this and
the examples we've been running.

24
00:01:57,503 --> 00:02:03,158
We've been running virtualbox,
running a virtual

25
00:02:03,158 --> 00:02:08,175
machine which runs
Ubuntu Linux inside of it.

26
00:02:08,175 --> 00:02:12,153
And I've been using vagrant
to kind of manage that.

27
00:02:12,153 --> 00:02:14,865
So we've been using that.

28
00:02:14,865 --> 00:02:17,063
But the problem is that it's heavyweight.

29
00:02:17,063 --> 00:02:20,467
And so the kind of question arose is what

30
00:02:20,467 --> 00:02:24,706
if we don't need different
operating systems?

31
00:02:24,706 --> 00:02:28,855
So the solution there was,
can we take advantage of that?

32
00:02:28,855 --> 00:02:35,052
And let's introduce isolation mechanisms
into the operating system itself.

33
00:02:35,052 --> 00:02:38,452
And so we've talked about namespaces,

34
00:02:38,452 --> 00:02:42,252
which are resources that
basically make it so

35
00:02:42,252 --> 00:02:47,853
that what resources and
naming of those resources a process sees.

36
00:02:47,853 --> 00:02:54,220
So this is like file descriptors,
IP addresses and things like that.

37
00:02:54,220 --> 00:03:00,434
So that helps with isolation
of the naming of resources.

38
00:03:00,434 --> 00:03:06,008
And then we have c groups or
control groups, which group processes and

39
00:03:06,008 --> 00:03:12,635
allocates resources, meaning like CPU and
memory that the kernel then enforces.

40
00:03:12,635 --> 00:03:19,408
All right, so those provide these
isolation mechanisms that we need.

41
00:03:19,408 --> 00:03:24,191
And we've seen this in all of our labs.

42
00:03:24,191 --> 00:03:26,597
So we've been using container lab.

43
00:03:26,597 --> 00:03:29,993
So we've had a container
lab configuration file.

44
00:03:29,993 --> 00:03:35,444
Then we've run container lab deploy,
which created docker containers.

45
00:03:35,444 --> 00:03:40,806
And so
we've seen an example like this before.

46
00:03:40,806 --> 00:03:45,163
And then we can do docker exec to run

47
00:03:45,163 --> 00:03:49,828
commands inside of each container.

48
00:03:49,828 --> 00:03:54,885
And in our labs and
our demos inside of this router we were

49
00:03:54,885 --> 00:04:02,318
setting up things like IP forwarding or
using iptables to set up filtering rules.

50
00:04:02,318 --> 00:04:07,335
And then we were able to ping
between different hosts as if

51
00:04:07,335 --> 00:04:14,861
they were completely different machines
because this router had its own namespace,

52
00:04:14,861 --> 00:04:19,478
so it had its own ability
to do things in networking.

53
00:04:19,478 --> 00:04:25,050
So a namespace, it just is
a mechanism that creates isolation in

54
00:04:25,050 --> 00:04:32,079
the kernel that allows processes to have
its own namespace for those resources.

55
00:04:32,079 --> 00:04:33,850
So let's look at what this means.

56
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:37,317
So this is some code
from the Linux kernel.

57
00:04:37,317 --> 00:04:42,144
So down here is a link to
where you can find this

58
00:04:42,144 --> 00:04:46,973
code in one of the online
source code browsers

59
00:04:46,973 --> 00:04:51,949
that are available, really great resource.

60
00:04:51,949 --> 00:04:54,106
As I've said stated before,

61
00:04:54,106 --> 00:04:58,175
we're not going to become
kernel hackers in this course.

62
00:04:58,175 --> 00:05:00,637
Even if you don't know c code,

63
00:05:00,637 --> 00:05:06,533
you probably could still understand
what this code is actually showing.

64
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:14,915
So inside of the Linux kernel it maintains
data structures on a per process basis.

65
00:05:14,915 --> 00:05:21,566
So we can see one of the key data
structures is called a task struct.

66
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:24,324
So every process will have this.

67
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:28,444
Whenever you run a program
the Linux kernel is going to have

68
00:05:28,444 --> 00:05:32,400
one of these structures allocated for
that process.

69
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:35,146
And there's a whole bunch of stuff in it.

70
00:05:35,146 --> 00:05:42,789
The main thing that I want to call out was
this particular variable called nsproxy.

71
00:05:42,789 --> 00:05:48,863
So it's of type struct nsproxy and
it's a pointer to that.

72
00:05:48,863 --> 00:05:54,362
So this right here is showing that
it's associated with the process.

73
00:05:54,362 --> 00:05:59,010
So now if we dive into that
structure a little bit,

74
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:03,770
the structure itself contains
a bunch of different

75
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:08,432
things that are all saying
something namespace.

76
00:06:08,432 --> 00:06:15,766
So this is the interprocess communication,
the mounting the process ids.

77
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:22,635
Well, low and behold we have
something called struct net and

78
00:06:22,635 --> 00:06:29,517
the variable name is called net_ns for
network namespace.

79
00:06:29,517 --> 00:06:35,044
All right, so the process has one of
these structures allocated which then

80
00:06:35,044 --> 00:06:41,011
contains one of these structures which
shows there's a whole bunch of different

81
00:06:41,011 --> 00:06:46,821
namespaces for all the different types
of resources that we might care about.

82
00:06:46,821 --> 00:06:51,738
The one we're going to focus
on is the networking one.

83
00:06:51,738 --> 00:06:55,334
So if we dive into that structure,

84
00:06:55,334 --> 00:07:01,415
what you can see is there's
variables called like ipv4.

85
00:07:01,415 --> 00:07:06,677
And so this is of struct type netns_ipv4.

86
00:07:06,677 --> 00:07:11,860
We see another one where it says nf for
net filter.

87
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:16,956
So those are all those filtering and
address translation rules and

88
00:07:16,956 --> 00:07:20,147
this is handling all the stuff for ipv4.

89
00:07:20,147 --> 00:07:23,675
So the relevant structure is this net and

90
00:07:23,675 --> 00:07:29,608
it contains all these different
ones I didn't include all of them.

91
00:07:29,608 --> 00:07:36,235
But let's now look
inside of this ipv4 one.

92
00:07:36,235 --> 00:07:44,778
And so here we can see things that
we should kind of be familiar with.

93
00:07:44,778 --> 00:07:48,002
This again is just a snapshot or

94
00:07:48,002 --> 00:07:53,334
a small set of the lines of
code that are under this,

95
00:07:53,334 --> 00:08:00,279
but we can see kind of words that
are probably familiar to us, fib.

96
00:08:00,279 --> 00:08:03,035
This is the forwarding information base.

97
00:08:03,035 --> 00:08:07,862
This is the forwarding table that's
stored inside of the Linux kernel.

98
00:08:07,862 --> 00:08:12,501
So anytime we did things
like IP route add, well,

99
00:08:12,501 --> 00:08:16,707
we were adding entries into the fib,
right?

100
00:08:16,707 --> 00:08:20,110
And so that is what's getting stored.

101
00:08:20,110 --> 00:08:25,341
So you can see that now
associated with a given

102
00:08:25,341 --> 00:08:29,753
process is a given forwarding table.

103
00:08:29,753 --> 00:08:33,763
And so if you had a different process,

104
00:08:33,763 --> 00:08:38,024
it would have a different task struct and

105
00:08:38,024 --> 00:08:42,551
it can have a different forwarding table.

106
00:08:42,551 --> 00:08:49,099
So the end result of all that is that each
namespace gets its own set of tables.

107
00:08:49,099 --> 00:08:53,620
And we saw this in practice
where we saw in host1.

108
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:56,139
I can set what forwarding table entry.

109
00:08:56,139 --> 00:09:00,238
I did IP route add and
then if I went inside the router and

110
00:09:00,238 --> 00:09:04,624
looked at its routing table,
I wouldn't see that entry.

111
00:09:04,624 --> 00:09:07,578
And I can add my own entries or

112
00:09:07,578 --> 00:09:12,268
different entries into the router, right?

113
00:09:12,268 --> 00:09:15,434
So each of these was a container.

114
00:09:15,434 --> 00:09:22,388
Each of these had its own namespace,
which meant it had its own tables.

115
00:09:22,388 --> 00:09:27,201
So kind of wrapping it all up,
what we're kind of saying is

116
00:09:27,201 --> 00:09:31,428
we're providing the concept
of virtualization,

117
00:09:31,428 --> 00:09:38,407
which is basically creating that isolation
so you can have better use of resources,

118
00:09:38,407 --> 00:09:43,451
more efficient use of resources and
better deployability.

119
00:09:43,451 --> 00:09:50,009
because we can create these
isolated packages of deployments,

120
00:09:50,009 --> 00:09:55,001
we're going to use
namespaces in this manner.

121
00:09:55,001 --> 00:10:00,709
And this is just an illustration
showing a process tree we're

122
00:10:00,709 --> 00:10:06,208
getting its own view of networking,
its own, all of that.

123
00:10:06,208 --> 00:10:11,282
And then that's all running
on top of a physical host

124
00:10:11,282 --> 00:10:16,020
where you would have
an actual physical device.

125
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:20,400
So with that we're going to end here, and

126
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:26,391
then next time we'll dive
into the IP netns utility.

127
00:10:26,391 --> 00:10:27,585
All right, thanks.